name: OzWorld Ruleset
version: 1
owner: OzWorld
description: |
  Comprehensive rules and project context for the OzWorld C/SDL2/OpenGL engine,
  editor, demo apps, and server. Optimized for development on Linux/WSL2 with
  optional Windows cross-compile. Use this ruleset to understand structure,
  build/run workflows, coding standards, and module responsibilities across the
  repository.
  3D Game Engine Ruleset Description

Overview

This ruleset governs development for a 3D Game Engine designed to emulate the aesthetic and technical style of mid-late 1990s multiplayer games, with a primary focus on First-Person Shooter (FPS) and Role-Playing Game (RPG) genres. The engine prioritizes nostalgic gameplay mechanics, period-appropriate graphics, and robust multiplayer capabilities, optimized for software rendering using Cairo and SDL in a WSL2/VcXsrv environment.

Engine Focus





Mid-Late 90s Style: Captures the visual and gameplay essence of 1990s 3D games, including low-polygon models, pixelated textures, and simple lighting (e.g., basic point lights with diffuse shading). Emphasizes fast-paced, responsive gameplay inspired by classics like Doom, Quake, or Ultima.



Multiplayer Capability: Supports networked multiplayer for cooperative and competitive modes, with client-server or peer-to-peer architecture suitable for FPS deathmatches and RPG party-based quests.



FPS Genre:





Core mechanics: Fast movement (WASD, sprint), precise aiming (mouse look), gravity, and jump mechanics.



Features: Weapon switching, projectile-based combat, and map-based objectives (e.g., capture points, frags).



RPG Genre:





Core mechanics: Character progression (stats, skills), inventory management, and narrative-driven quests.



Features: Dialogue systems, NPC interactions, and open-world exploration with 90s-style level design (e.g., hub-based maps).



Technical Constraints: Optimized for software rendering (oz_demo_sw) using Cairo and SDL, avoiding OpenGL/GLX to ensure compatibility with VcXsrv/WSL2. Prioritizes performance on modest hardware, mimicking 90s-era constraints.



Editor Integration: Includes a built-in editor for level design, supporting nodes (player start, gizmo handles) and cinematic keyframe paths, rendered in software mode for consistency.

contexts:
  # Primary source and headers
  - @src/
  - @include/
  # Editor application (GTK3)
  - @editor/
  # Applications (demo, server)
  - @apps/
  # Generated build trees (referenced read-only as artifacts and for compile_commands)
  - @build/
  - @build-win/
  # Build and configuration
  - @cmake/
  - @Makefile
  - @CMakeLists.txt
  # Scripts and sample assets
  - @scripts/
  - @testozones/
  # Top-level documentation
  - @README.md

conventions:
  coding_language: C11
  formatting:
    # Match repository style; do not reformat unrelated code
    indent_style: preserve
    line_length: 100
  compile_settings:
    warnings_common: [-Wall, -Wextra, -Wshadow, -Wconversion, -Wstrict-prototypes, -Wpedantic]
    treat_warnings_as_errors: opt_in (OZ_WARNINGS_AS_ERRORS)
    math_link: link_m_on_unix
  build_system: CMake (Makefile shim)
  platforms:
    - linux
    - windows_cross (MinGW via cmake/toolchains)
  rendering_backends:
    - OpenGL (preferred when available)
    - Software (Cairo/X11 fallback)

repository_overview:
  summary: |
    OzWorld is a cross‑platform engine with core systems in `ozcore`, platform
    abstraction in `ozplatform_sdl_*`, rendering backends for GL and software,
    a GTK3 editor, demo apps, and a simple HTTP server. The editor loads/saves
    `*.ozone` maps (loads legacy `*.ozmap`), provides primitive brush creation
    (box, cylinder), and uses GtkGLArea when GL is available or a Cairo fallback
    when not.
  layout:
    - include/oz: Public headers for the engine API
    - src/core: Core engine modules (logging, core, bsp/map, camera, debug, assets, tex, bundles, audio)
    - src/render: Rendering shared code + GL and software backends
    - src/platform/sdl: SDL platform backends (GL and SW variants)
    - editor: GTK3-based editor wiring and UI
    - apps/oz_demo: SDL demo (GL or software based on availability)
    - apps/oz_demo_sw: Software-only demo
    - apps/oz_server: Simple UNIX HTTP server
    - cmake/toolchains: Toolchain files (e.g., MinGW for Windows)
    - scripts: Setup and helper scripts (Linux/WSL2, Windows build)

build:
  cmake:
    configure:
      linux: "cmake -S . -B build -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=RelWithDebInfo"
      windows_cross: "cmake -S . -B build-win -DCMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE=cmake/toolchains/mingw-w64.cmake -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=RelWithDebInfo"
    build:
      linux: "cmake --build build -j"
      windows_cross: "cmake --build build-win -j"
  makefile:
    default: "make"           # calls cmake configure+build with RelWithDebInfo
    debug:   "make debug"
    release: "make release"
    clean:   "make clean"
    run_demo:"make run"
  options:
    - OZ_BUILD_DEMO (ON by default)
    - OZ_BUILD_EDITOR (ON by default, requires GTK3)
    - OZ_BUILD_SERVER (ON on UNIX)
    - OZ_WITH_AUDIO (ON by default; SDL2_mixer if available)
    - OZ_WARNINGS_AS_ERRORS (OFF by default)

run:
  demo:
    binary: ./build/oz_demo
    notes: |
      Controls: WASD move, QE up/down, arrows rotate. Links GL backend when OpenGL
      is found, otherwise the software backend. For software-only build, use oz_demo_sw.
  demo_sw:
    binary: ./build/oz_demo_sw
  editor:
    binary: ./build/oz_editor
    file_menu: [Open, Save] for *.ozone
    fallback: |
      Uses GtkGLArea when GL is available. Falls back to Cairo software path when GLX
      is unavailable (e.g., WSL2 with X server). To force fallback: GDK_GL=disable ./build/oz_editor
  server:
    binary: ./build/oz_server
    endpoints:
      - GET /map?name=sample.ozone -> JSON
      - POST /auth/login -> dummy token

dependencies:
  discovery:
    sdl2: "find_package(SDL2) or pkg-config sdl2"
    opengl: "find_package(OpenGL)"
    gtk3: "pkg-config gtk+-3.0"
    sdl2_mixer: "find_package(SDL2_mixer) or pkg-config SDL2_mixer (when OZ_WITH_AUDIO)"
    cairo: "pkg-config cairo (and Cairo::Cairo fallback)"
    x11: "find_package(X11 REQUIRED) for software backend"
  link_notes:
    - Link libm on UNIX targets using trig/math (ozcore, demos, editor)
    - When OpenGL is absent, prefer software targets (ozplatform_sdl_sw, oz_demo_sw)

editor_guidance:
  ui:
    toolkit: GTK3
    gl_area: GtkGLArea when available; detect and degrade gracefully
    software_view: Cairo/X11 path for non-GL environments
  features:
    - Open/Save .ozone (load legacy .ozmap)
    - Add primitive brushes: Box, Cylinder dialogs
    - Live viewport rendering
  wsl2_tips:
    xserver: "Use VcXsrv; disable Native OpenGL; set DISPLAY and LIBGL_ALWAYS_INDIRECT"
    fallback: "Use software viewport or run natively if GLX is unavailable"

rendering_guidance:
  gl_backend:
    files: [src/render/oz_render_gl.c]
    link: OpenGL::GL when available; otherwise do not link GL targets
  software_backend:
    files: [src/render/oz_render_soft.c]
    link: X11::X11 and Cairo
  shared:
    files: [src/render/oz_render_common.c]

core_modules:
  - oz_log: src/core/oz_log.c
  - oz_core: src/core/oz_core.c
  - oz_bsp: src/core/oz_bsp.c (primitive brush map model, save/load .ozone)
  - oz_camera: src/core/oz_camera.c (free-move camera for demos)
  - oz_debug: src/core/oz_debug.c
  - oz_assets: src/core/oz_assets.c
  - oz_tex: src/core/oz_tex.c (texture/oztex helpers)
  - oz_bundle: src/core/oz_bundle.c (asset bundles .ozbag)
  - oz_audio_sdlmixer: src/core/oz_audio_sdlmixer.c (optional audio)

map_format:
  ozone_v1:
    header: "OZONE 1"
    primitives:
      - box: "box cx cy cz sx sy sz"
      - cylinder: "cyl cx cy cz rx ry h segments"
    legacy_load: "OZMAP 1"

coding_standards:
  c:
    standard: C11
    guidelines:
      - Prefer meaningful identifiers; avoid 1–2 char names
      - Use guard clauses; handle error/edge cases first
      - Limit nesting to 2–3 levels; prefer early returns
      - Avoid catch‑all error suppression; propagate or log via oz_log
      - Keep comments focused on intent; avoid trivial comments
      - Avoid TODOs; implement or create a tracked issue
      - Match existing formatting; do not mass‑reformat unrelated code
      - Keep lines reasonably short and readable
    warnings:
      enforce: [-Wall, -Wextra, -Wshadow, -Wconversion, -Wstrict-prototypes, -Wpedantic]
    errors:
      prefer_runtime_checks: true
      never_ignore_return_values: true
  threading:
    model: single-threaded by default
    note: Add synchronization primitives explicitly if introducing threads

contribution_workflows:
  feature_addition:
    - Update core API under include/oz and corresponding src/core implementation
    - Integrate with rendering backends as needed (GL and/or software)
    - Expose capability in demo and/or editor where appropriate
    - Extend CMakeLists.txt targets and link dependencies explicitly
  platform_changes:
    - Keep ozplatform_sdl_gl free of software‑only deps; GL only
    - Keep ozplatform_sdl_sw free of GL/GLX calls; Cairo/X11 only
  windows_cross_compile:
    - Use toolchain: cmake/toolchains/mingw-w64.cmake -> build-win
    - Prefer pkg-config discovery for SDL2/SDL2_mixer when cross‑compiling

troubleshooting:
  build:
    deps: "sudo apt install build-essential cmake pkg-config libsdl2-dev libgtk-3-dev mesa-utils libgl1-mesa-dev"
    math_link: Ensure -lm on UNIX (handled by CMake)
  editor_gl:
    issue: "No available configurations for the given RGBA pixel format"
    actions:
      - Use software fallback (Cairo) or force via GDK_GL=disable
      - On WSL2, ensure X server running and GLX configured; disable Native OpenGL in VcXsrv
  sdl_demo:
    window_black: Ensure X server is running; verify DISPLAY; consider vsync off

context_index:
  - path: @src/
    purpose: Engine core, rendering backends, platform layer, editor sources
  - path: @include/
    purpose: Public API headers
  - path: @editor/
    purpose: GTK3-based editor entry and glue code
  - path: @apps/
    purpose: Demo apps and server
  - path: @build/
    purpose: Build artifacts and compile_commands.json (read-only)
  - path: @build-win/
    purpose: Windows cross-compiled artifacts (read-only)
  - path: @cmake/
    purpose: CMake helpers and toolchains
  - path: @scripts/
    purpose: Setup and helper scripts (WSL2, Windows)
  - path: @testozones/
    purpose: Sample ozone maps for testing
  - path: @Makefile
    purpose: Make shim for CMake build
  - path: @README.md
    purpose: Project overview, quick start, troubleshooting
  - path: @CMakeLists.txt
    purpose: Build targets, options, dependency discovery

rules:
  - Prefer software rendering targets when GL is not available (or on remote X)
  - Keep GL and SW backends isolated; never mix GLX calls into software path
  - Ensure new math usage links libm on UNIX targets
  - Update public headers alongside core implementations; maintain API consistency
  - Add CMake target link dependencies explicitly; avoid transitive leakage
  - When adding editor features, ensure both GL and software view paths render
  - Preserve indentation style and width of existing files during edits
  - Avoid introducing noisy formatting diffs; scope edits minimally

ue_inspired_guidance:
  purpose: |
    Apply proven architectural and development patterns inspired by Unreal Engine's
    modular design, lifecycle, and coding discipline, adapted to OzWorld's C/CMake
    codebase and SDL/GL/Software backends. This guidance is conceptual; implement
    incrementally where it adds clarity, safety, and scalability.
  architecture:
    modules_and_boundaries:
      - Keep clear boundaries between core, platform, render, editor, and apps.
      - Mirror the idea of independent modules that initialize and shut down in
        a well-defined order. Each module should expose a small public API under
        `include/oz` and own its internal state in `src/...`.
      - Avoid leaking platform specifics across layers (akin to Unreal's RHI abstraction):
        hide OpenGL/Cairo/X11 behind render backends; expose a minimal render API
        in shared render code where practical.
    initialization_order:
      - core/logging -> platform (SDL init) -> render backend (GL or SW) -> assets ->
        world/map -> application/editor. Fail fast if any stage cannot initialize.
    update_model:
      - Adopt coarse-grained phases inspired by Unreal's tick pipeline where useful:
        PreUpdate (input/events) -> SimUpdate (gameplay/physics) -> PostUpdate (cleanup)
        -> RenderSubmit -> Present. Map these to the demo/editor main loops.
    configuration:
      - Centralize feature toggles and build-time options (akin to Build.cs) via
        CMake options and `oz_config.h`. Keep compile-time switches out of call sites.
  logging_and_assertions:
    categories:
      - Use categorized logging (e.g., core, render, platform, editor, net) via `oz_log`.
      - Prefer structured messages that include identifiers (map name, entity id, file path).
    assertions:
      - Treat invariants as fatal: assert in debug builds for programmer errors.
      - Use runtime checks for recoverable conditions; log at appropriate severity.
      - Guard expensive debug checks with a debug macro if available (e.g., `OZ_DEBUG`).
  threading_and_jobs:
    model:
      - Default remains single-threaded. If introducing threads (e.g., async asset IO),
        ensure thread-safe queues and avoid calling render backend from worker threads.
      - Define clear ownership and lifetime for data shared across threads.
    timing:
      - Keep render submission on the main thread; use background decode/IO where safe.
  networking_and_replication:
    authority_model:
      - Server-authoritative mindset: server validates state; clients predict locally
        only for responsiveness in demos if added later.
    ids_and_serialization:
      - Use stable identifiers for entities/resources over the wire. Version payloads
        and include `map_format` versioning in responses.
    time_sync:
      - Include basic time delta and sequence numbers to handle latency/out-of-order.
  assets_and_content:
    packages_and_paths:
      - Keep asset packages (`.ozbag`) self-contained with explicit references; avoid
        implicit global state. Use forward slashes and lowercase paths.
    editor_only:
      - Isolate editor-only code under `editor/` and exclude from runtime targets.
    hot_reload:
      - Support safe reload where feasible by reinitializing resources at module boundaries.
  naming_and_style:
    types_functions:
      - Prefer descriptive snake_case for functions and variables (consistent with OzWorld).
      - Use clear, domain-centric names; avoid abbreviations and 1–2 character identifiers.
    files_and_targets:
      - Keep one primary responsibility per file. Name targets and files by feature area
        (`oz_render_soft.c`, `oz_bsp.c`, etc.).
  memory_and_resources:
    ownership:
      - Provide explicit init/free pairs for heap-allocated resources. Document ownership
        in the function names and headers. Zero-initialize on creation.
    lifetime:
      - Tie resource lifetime to module or world lifetime. Avoid hidden singletons; pass
        required context explicitly.
    pooling:
      - For hot paths (render vertices, temp buffers), consider simple arenas/pools with
        clear reset points at frame boundaries.
  error_handling:
    policy:
      - Fail fast on programmer errors; log and recover when input/data is untrusted.
      - Propagate errors up; avoid silent fallbacks except where explicitly intended
        (e.g., GL -> software backend).
  build_and_dependencies:
    targets:
      - Model dependency hygiene after modular build systems: each CMake target declares
        its include dirs and link deps; avoid relying on transitive includes.
    discovery:
      - Keep third-party discovery in top-level `CMakeLists.txt` and pass minimal
        interface to dependents via target_link_libraries.
  testing_and_validation:
    maps:
      - Maintain small `.ozone` test maps focused on single features (lighting, BSP,
        camera, IO). Add new ones alongside features.
    demos:
      - Add minimal demo modes for new subsystems to exercise lifecycle and performance.

